Determine whether the function $f(x) = x \cos x$ satisfies the hypotheses of the Mean Value Theorem on the interval $[0, 2\pi]$. If it does, find all numbers $c$ that satisfy the conclusion of the theorem. If it does not, explain why. To determine whether $f(x) = x \cos x$ satisfies the hypotheses of the Mean Value Theorem on the interval $[0, 2\pi]$, we need to verify two conditions: continuity of $f(x)$ and differentiability of $f(x)$ on the interval. Since $f(x)$ is differentiable on all of $\mathbb{R}$, it is continuous and differentiable on the closed interval $[0, 2\pi]$. To find values $c$ such that $f'(c) = \frac{f(2\pi) - f(0)}{2\pi - 0}$, we compute the following:

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx} \left( x \cos x \right) \\
&= x \cdot (-\sin x) + \cos x \\
&= \cos x - x \sin x
\end{align*}

Also, $f(0) = 0$ and $f(2\pi) = 2\pi \cos(2\pi) = 2\pi$, so according to the MVT, there must exist some $c$ such that

\begin{align*}
f'(c) &= \frac{2\pi - 0}{2\pi - 0} \\
\cos c - c \sin c &= 1
\end{align*}

